Compatibility
    The Telegram-derived code is offered under GPL-2.0-or-later, which allows the combined
    distribution under GPL-3.0. This reading has not been reviewed by a lawyer. Whether the
    combined build may be distributed is the owner's decision.

    MPL-2.0 files (libdatachannel, libjuice). This is an engineering record of a check, not a legal opinion.
    A search of third-party/libdatachannel for the notice "Incompatible With Secondary Licenses" found no
    source file that carries it. The term appears only in the two MPL-2.0 licence texts. Section 3.3 of
    MPL-2.0 lets a Larger Work that combines such Covered Software with a work under a Secondary License
    (GPL-2.0-or-later is one) also distribute the Covered Software under that Secondary License, unless the
    Covered Software is marked Incompatible With Secondary Licenses.
    Open point: no source file carries the MPL-2.0 Exhibit A notice (a search for "Mozilla Public License"
    finds it only in the two LICENSE files). Section 1.3 defines Covered Software by reference to that notice,
    so the MPL terms reach these files through the LICENSE files. Whether that is enough is for the owner to decide.
